{1 Assembler}


The assembler's job is to take programs written in the [Gaur ASM] assembly language and translate them to one of three formats:

- [inter]: Intermediate [Gaur ASM] programs, with all the labels resolved and only the base [Gaur ISA] instructions (no pseudo-instructions).
- [ascii]: ASCII representations of [Gaur ISA] binaries, where each instruction is written in it's own line with 16-character strings consisting of [0]s and [1]s, corresponding to the bit-wise layout of the instruction.
- [bin]: [Gaur ISA] binaries.

{2 Web tool}

The web version of assembler can be found {{:../tools/assembler.html} here}


{2 Usage}

The [assembler] executable can be invoked with various flags. To see an exhaustive list of the same, invoke it with the [--help] flag. The output will be something like this:

{v
λ⟩ assembler --help
Usage: assembler [options] <file_path>.
  --format 
        Output format (bin | inter | ascii)
  -fmt 
        Short for --format
  --out 
        Output file path
  -o 
        Short for --out
  --help 
        Display this list of options
  -help 
        Display this list of options
  -h 
        Short for --help / -help
v}

{2 Exit Codes}

{t
  | Exit Code | Meaning |
  | :-------: | :-----: |
  | 0 | Exited successfully |
  | 1 | Command-line issues: more or less arguments than expected, invalid flag passed, etc. |
  | 2 | Lexing or parsing errors |
  | 3 | Assembling errors |
}

Note that errors for instructions having immediate arguments outside their valid range of immediates (such as [addi r0 r0 420]) are flagged as "Lexing or parsing errors", as it is the parser that actually catches these errors.


{2 Example}

The [examples/] directory in the source code contains a [Gaur ASM] program to place the first [n] Fibonacci numbers in the memory, starting at address [k], where the initial value of [RAM[0]] contains [n], and that of [RAM[1]] contains [k].

{v
# fib.gsm

# Let RAM[0] store n and RAM[1] store k.
# This program computes the first n Fibonacci numbers and puts them
# in RAM, starting at address k.


ld r1 r0 0
ld r2 r0 1

addi r3 r0 0
addi r5 r0 0
addi r6 r0 1

loop:
	beq r3 r1 halt
 	add r4 r2 r3
 	st 	r5 r4 0
 	add r6 r6 r5
 	sub r5 r6 r5
 	addi r3 r3 1
 	j loop
 
halt:
	j halt

v}


To assemble this and see the ASCII representation of the [Gaur ISA] binary, the [assembler] executable can be invoked as follows:

{v
λ⟩ assembler fib.gsm -fmt=ascii -o fib-ascii
Wrote: fib-ascii

λ⟩ cat fib-ascii
0010000100000000
0010001000000001
0101001100000000
0101010100000000
0101011000000001
1001001100010111
0100010000100011
0011010101000000
0100011001100101
0110010101100101
0101001100110001
1101111111111010
1101000000000000
v}


{2 Source code and documentation}

The source code for the assembler can be found on our {{:https://gitlab.com/verify-at-iitpkd/gaur/} GitLab repository}, in the [assembler] directory.

The [odoc] generated documentation for the libraries involved can be read here:

- {!Assembler.Types}: [OCaml] types for [Gaur ASM] and [Gaur ISA] instructions and programs. The extraction script maps types in the [Rocq] theories to these types.
- {!Assembler.Printers}: Pretty printing functions for the types in {!Assembler.Types}.
- {!Assembler.Parse}: Functions to parse strings to [Gaur ASM] and [Gaur ISA] ASTs. These functions are wrappers over those generated by [ocamllex] and [Menhir].
- {!Assembler.Assemble}: Functions to assemble [Gaur ASM] programs into the various formats. These are wrappers over functions generated by the [Rocq] to [OCaml] extraction.
- Rocq theories: {i Coming soon...}

